  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-8t+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(2)^{3}-4(2)^{2}+4(2)+C \\\\ 0 & =8-16+8+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t=t(t-2)^{2}>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/9254ea32fc55b2a703e52f55f4292d9708eef63ae147a9f1d2eac40b85eff319.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<2$ or $t>2.$",
